  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nNot a whole lot of interesting developments in the long term from \nprevious forecasts. A weak NW flow regime is in place while an upper \nlevel low bats back the ridge to our south. The weaker ridging \npattern will allow temperatures to lower a tad Wednesday through the \nend of the forecast period, which is a plus. Highs in the 80s east \nand 70s out west. Lows in the 50s and 40s. Humidity values stay low, \nin the low 20s and teens and winds start increasing ahead of the \nnext Pacific trough. Global models are starting to come into focus \non this system. The evolution is still uncertain, particularly on \nhow compact and amplified this trough enters the scene, but the \npresence of this system is becoming more certain. A compact system \nwould bring greater southwesterly winds. Not the greatest news when \ncombined with the dry afternoon humidities for elevated fire \nconcerns next weekend.\n\nThe ensembles start diverging greatly on Sunday. A cold \nfront in some of the solutions is driving a nearly 10 to 25 degree \nspread in ensemble members. A good cold front and Pacific system \nwould bring in rain and storms. Temperatures would still be on the \nwarm side for snow, outside of mountains.",
